@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "ARM Templates vs Terraform";
    ViewData["ActiveMenu"] = "AzureDevOps";
}

<div class="container py-4">
    <p class="lead text-center">Comparing Azure Resource Manager (ARM) Templates and Terraform for infrastructure automation.</p>
    <hr />

    <!-- Introduction -->
    <div class="mt-4">
        <h2 class="text-success">Introduction to Infrastructure as Code</h2>
        <p>
            Infrastructure as Code (IaC) is a key DevOps practice that allows teams to define and manage infrastructure through machine-readable definition files.
            Two popular IaC tools for Azure are ARM Templates and Terraform.
        </p>
        <div class="text-center my-3">
            <img src="https://example.com/iac-overview.jpg" alt="Infrastructure as Code" class="img-fluid rounded shadow-lg w-100">
            <p class="text-muted mt-2"><small>IaC simplifies infrastructure provisioning and management.</small></p>
        </div>
    </div>

    <!-- What are ARM Templates? -->
    <div class="mt-5">
        <h2 class="text-warning">What are ARM Templates?</h2>
        <p>
            Azure Resource Manager (ARM) Templates are JSON-based files that define the resources and configurations for deploying infrastructure in Microsoft Azure.
            They provide declarative syntax to describe Azure resources and their dependencies.
        </p>
        <ul class="list-group list-group-flush">
            <li class="list-group-item">Microsoft-native infrastructure deployment tool.</li>
            <li class="list-group-item">JSON-based declarative format.</li>
            <li class="list-group-item">Integrated with Azure Resource Manager.</li>
            <li class="list-group-item">Supports parameterization and modular templates.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <!-- What is Terraform? -->
    <div class="mt-5">
        <h2 class="text-info">What is Terraform?</h2>
        <p>
            Terraform is an open-source infrastructure as code tool developed by HashiCorp. It supports multiple cloud providers, including Azure, AWS, and Google Cloud.
            Terraform uses HashiCorp Configuration Language (HCL) to define infrastructure declaratively.
        </p>
        <ul class="list-group list-group-flush">
            <li class="list-group-item">Cloud-agnostic and supports multi-cloud deployments.</li>
            <li class="list-group-item">HCL syntax, which is more readable than JSON.</li>
            <li class="list-group-item">State management for tracking infrastructure changes.</li>
            <li class="list-group-item">Supports modules for reusable infrastructure components.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <!-- Key Differences -->
    <div class="mt-5">
        <h2 class="text-danger">Key Differences: ARM Templates vs Terraform</h2>
        <table class="table table-bordered">
            <thead>
                <tr class="bg-light">
                    <th>Feature</th>
                    <th>ARM Templates</th>
                    <th>Terraform</th>
                </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr>
                    <td>Supported Platforms</td>
                    <td>Azure Only</td>
                    <td>Multi-Cloud (Azure, AWS, GCP, etc.)</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>Syntax</td>
                    <td>JSON-based</td>
                    <td>HCL-based (HashiCorp Configuration Language)</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>State Management</td>
                    <td>No built-in state management</td>
                    <td>Maintains state to track changes</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>Reusability</td>
                    <td>Supports modular templates</td>
                    <td>Uses modules for better reusability</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>Execution Model</td>
                    <td>Push-based (directly applies changes)</td>
                    <td>Plan and apply model (preview changes before applying)</td>
                </tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>

    <!-- When to Use What? -->
    <div class="mt-5">
        <h2 class="text-primary">When to Use ARM Templates vs Terraform</h2>
        <p>
            Choosing between ARM Templates and Terraform depends on your project requirements. Consider the following:
        </p>
        <ul class="list-group list-group-flush">
            <li class="list-group-item"><strong>Use ARM Templates</strong> if your infrastructure is Azure-only and you want deep integration with Azure features.</li>
            <li class="list-group-item"><strong>Use Terraform</strong> if you need a cloud-agnostic solution with better modularity and state management.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <!-- Conclusion -->
    <div class="mt-5">
        <h2 class="text-secondary">Conclusion</h2>
        <p>
            Both ARM Templates and Terraform provide powerful capabilities for infrastructure automation in Azure. While ARM Templates offer native integration, Terraform provides flexibility across multiple clouds.
            The choice between them depends on your specific use case and infrastructure strategy.
        </p>
        <div class="text-center my-3">
            <img src="https://example.com/terraform-vs-arm.jpg" alt="ARM Templates vs Terraform" class="img-fluid rounded shadow-lg w-100">
            <p class="text-muted mt-2"><small>Choosing the right IaC tool depends on your cloud strategy.</small></p>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>
